<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-11" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Intro to Energy</title>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Two spherical asteroids of masses <m>m, M</m> somehow have no velocity
            relative to one another, and their centers are separated a distance <m>r</m>
            from one another.
            They also happen to have the same radius <m>R</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What is <alert>the system</alert> for which you can say the
                        total energy <m>K+U</m> is conserved?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Is energy the only conserved quantity in this system?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Draw a picture of what the system looks like when the asteroids
                        hit each other.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use your picture + the conservation laws to set up a system of
                        equations that lets you solve for their speeds when they
                        collide.
                        Note: to calculate the potential energy <m>U</m>, use the
                        center-to-center distance between the asteroids.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        Consider a rock interacting gravitationally with the Earth, 
        and choose the interaction potential energy to be zero if the rock is 
        at the ground level. A person standing at the bottom of a well 
        throws the rock vertically upward from 20 m below ground level. 
        The rock makes it all the way up to 1 m below ground level before 
        falling back into the well. What is the total energy of the 
        rock-Earth system?
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        Consider the Atwood machine shown in the figure below. 
        Our system of interest will be the two masses and the Earth
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="70% 30%">
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The external interaction with our system in this case comes from the
                    tension in the rope.
                    Show that the tension does no net work on the system
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Set the potential energy <m>U=0</m> in the figure and find the
                    potential energy <m>U(z)</m>, where <m>z</m> is the displacement of
                    the heavier mass, taken to be positive <alert>going down</alert>
                    (use the constraint here, you don't have to be super rigorous about
                    it).
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Discussion:</alert> why can we say that the kinetic energy of
                    this system is <m>K=\frac{1}{2}(m+M)\dot{z}^{2}</m>?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The energy <m>E=K+U</m> is constant.
                    Show that setting <m>\dfrac{dE}{dt}= 0</m> let's you solve for the
                    acceleration <m>~{\ddot{z}=\dfrac{M-m}{m+M}g}</m>.
                    Lesson: conservation of energy fully determines the motion of a
                    system that is effectively one-dimensional.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <image source="images\activities\lec11\atwood.png">
            <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
        </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
